      "text": "Given that the polynomial \\( ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d = 0 \\) has roots \\( 3 \\) and \\( -2 \\), and the coefficients are in terms of \\( a \\), we can express the polynomial as:\n\\[\na(x - 3)(x + 2)(x - r) = 0\n\\]\nwhere \\( r \\) is the third root. Expanding this product, we start with the binomials:\n\\[\n(x - 3)(x + 2) = x^2 - x - 6\n\\]\nNext, we multiply this result by the third binomial:\n\\[\n(x^2 - x - 6)(x - r) = x^3 - rx^2 - x^2 + rx - 6x + 6r = x^3 - (r+1)x^2 + (r-6)x + 6r\n\\]\nThus, the polynomial can be written as:\n\\[\na(x^3 - (r+1)x^2 + (r-6)x + 6r) = ax^3 - a(r+1)x^2 + a(r-6)x + 6ar\n\\]\nBy comparing this with the original polynomial \\( ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d \\), we identify the coefficients:\n\\[\nb = -a(r+1), \\quad c = a(r-6), \\quad d = 6ar\n\\]\nWe need to compute \\( \\frac{b+c}{a} \\). First, we find \\( b + c \\):\n\\[\nb + c = -a(r+1) + a(r-6) = -ar - a + ar - 6a = -a - 6a = -7a\n\\]\nNow, we divide \\( b + c \\) by \\( a \\):\n\\[\n\\frac{b+c}{a} = \\frac{-7a}{a} = -7\n\\]\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-7}\n\\]",
