      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "We are given that the roots of the polynomial equation \\(ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d = 0\\) are 3 and -2. Since the roots of a polynomial with real coefficients come in conjugate pairs if they are complex, we can infer that the third root must also be real. Let's denote the third root by \\(r\\).\n\nGiven the roots \\(3\\), \\(-2\\), and \\(r\\), we can express the polynomial in its factored form as:\n\\[\na(x - 3)(x + 2)(x - r)\n\\]\n\nFirst, we expand the product \\((x - 3)(x + 2)\\):\n\\[\n(x - 3)(x + 2) = x^2 + 2x - 3x - 6 = x^2 - x - 6\n\\]\n\nNext, we multiply this result by \\((x - r)\\):\n\\[\n(x^2 - x - 6)(x - r) = x^3 - rx^2 - x^2 + rx - 6x + 6r = x^3 - (r+1)x^2 + (r-6)x + 6r\n\\]\n\nThus, the polynomial \\(ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d\\) can be written as:\n\\[\na(x^3 - (r+1)x^2 + (r-6)x + 6r)\n\\]\n\nBy comparing coefficients with the general form \\(ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d\\), we identify:\n\\[\nb = -a(r+1), \\quad c = a(r-6), \\quad d = 6ar\n\\]\n\nWe need to find \\(\\frac{b + c}{a}\\):\n\\[\nb + c = -a(r+1) + a(r-6) = -ar - a + ar - 6a = -7a\n\\]\n\nTherefore:\n\\[\n\\frac{b + c}{a} = \\frac{-7a}{a} = -7\n\\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-7}\n\\]",
